<p>Here's the short version, because it's the thing most people searching this actually need to know: a cat cannot catch your cold, and you cannot catch a cat's cold. The viruses are different species of virus entirely. What people call a "cat cold" is almost always feline upper respiratory infection, and the two main culprits, feline herpesvirus-1 and feline calicivirus, are specific to cats. Human rhinoviruses, the ones behind our colds, don't infect cats, and feline herpesvirus and calicivirus don't infect us.</p>

<p>That's not a guess. The Cornell Feline Health Center's page on feline respiratory infections lists which of these pathogens can cross between species, and herpesvirus and calicivirus, the two responsible for the vast majority of feline URI cases, aren't on that list. Cornell does flag a handful of other, much rarer respiratory agents in cats, like avian influenza and canine influenza, as capable of crossing species lines, and notes that Bordetella bronchiseptica can very rarely pass to people. But the common "cat cold" isn't caused by any of those. It's caused by viruses that only make cats sick.</p>

<p>So if you've been feeling run down and your cat started sneezing a few days later, it's a coincidence, not contagion. And if your cat has gunky eyes and you're worried about catching it, you can relax on that front too, though it's still smart to wash your hands after wiping her face, mostly to protect other cats she might go near.</p>

<h2>So What Is a "Cat Cold," Exactly?</h2>

<p>Veterinarians call it feline upper respiratory infection, or URI, and sometimes feline viral rhinotracheitis when herpesvirus is the specific cause. VCA Animal Hospitals' overview of the condition puts the numbers plainly: feline herpesvirus-1 and feline calicivirus together cause roughly 90% of cases. The rest come from bacteria like Bordetella bronchiseptica and Chlamydophila felis, with mycoplasma and feline reovirus as less common players.</p>

<p>It spreads the way you'd expect a respiratory bug to spread between cats in close quarters: direct contact, sneezed droplets, and shared bowls, litter boxes, toys, or bedding. VCA notes that calicivirus is unusually hardy and can survive on surfaces for up to ten days, while herpesvirus dies off faster, within about 18 hours outside a host. Both are killed by diluted bleach, which is part of why shelters and multi-cat households clean so aggressively during an outbreak.</p>

<h2>What It Actually Looks Like</h2>

<p>The symptoms overlap a lot with a human cold, which is exactly why owners reach for that comparison:</p>

<ul>
<li>Sneezing, sometimes in fits</li>
<li>Clear or thicker discharge from the nose and eyes</li>
<li>Nasal congestion that can make breathing sound stuffy or noisy</li>
<li>Conjunctivitis, or visibly inflamed, watery, squinty eyes</li>
<li>Reduced appetite, often because a congested nose means a cat can't smell her food, and cats rely heavily on smell to decide whether something is worth eating</li>
<li>Lethargy, mild fever, and sometimes swollen lymph nodes</li>
</ul>

<p>One symptom that doesn't show up in human colds is worth flagging specifically: mouth ulcers. VCA lists them as a feature of both herpesvirus and calicivirus infections, and calicivirus in particular is associated with oral ulceration. A cat who's drooling, pawing at her mouth, or suddenly reluctant to eat hard food, on top of the sneezing and discharge, may have ulcers rather than just a sore throat. We've covered the broader list of reasons cats drool, including this one, in <a href="/blog/why-is-my-cat-drooling/">our piece on cat drooling</a>, if that's a bigger concern for your cat right now than the respiratory signs are.</p>

<p>Eye involvement deserves its own mention too. Herpesvirus in particular can cause corneal ulcers, not just conjunctivitis, and corneal ulcers can scar or threaten vision if they go untreated. Squinting, a cloudy-looking cornea, or a cat who won't open one eye fully isn't something to wait out at home.</p>

<h2>Mild and Self-Limiting, or Something That Needs a Vet?</h2>

<p>Most uncomplicated cases in otherwise healthy adult cats run their course with supportive care at home, similar to how a mild human cold does. VCA's guidance treats home management as the default for straightforward cases, and antibiotics aren't used against the viral causes themselves, since antibiotics don't touch viruses. They're sometimes prescribed anyway to head off secondary bacterial infection, particularly in kittens, whose immune systems have less margin for error.</p>

<p>That margin for error is the key variable. Kittens, senior cats, and cats with other health problems, like diabetes, kidney disease, or FIV, are at meaningfully higher risk of a case that turns serious. In those groups, a cat cold can progress to pneumonia, severe dehydration from not eating or drinking, or eye damage that becomes permanent if it isn't caught early.</p>

<p>Signs that mean a vet visit rather than home care, regardless of the cat's age or history:</p>

<ul>
<li>Labored or open-mouth breathing, or breathing that looks like real effort rather than a stuffy nose</li>
<li>Not eating or drinking for more than a day</li>
<li>Visible eye pain, cloudiness, or a cat keeping one eye shut</li>
<li>Lethargy that goes beyond "sleeping more than usual"</li>
<li>Thick, colored, or foul-smelling discharge, which points toward a secondary bacterial infection</li>
<li>Symptoms that haven't started improving within a week to ten days, which is roughly the typical course for an uncomplicated case</li>
</ul>

<p>If your cat has stopped eating because of nasal congestion, our <a href="/tools/cat-not-eating-guide/">cat not eating guide</a> covers how to tell ordinary pickiness from the kind of appetite loss that needs medical attention, which matters here because a cat who refuses food for more than a day or two, especially a kitten or a cat with another illness, is at real risk of a separate problem: hepatic lipidosis, a liver condition that can develop from not eating at all. If you're ever unsure whether a symptom at home counts as an emergency, our <a href="/tools/pet-first-aid-guide/">pet first aid guide</a> is a reasonable place to check before you decide whether to call the vet that day or wait until morning.</p>

<h2>What Actually Helps at Home</h2>

<p>For a mild case your vet has already looked at, or while you're waiting to get in, there are a few things that genuinely make a difference, and they're the same things VCA recommends:</p>

<p>Gently wipe discharge from the nose and eyes with a warm, damp tissue or cloth a few times a day. Crusted discharge blocks the nostrils further and makes breathing and smelling harder, so keeping the face clean helps on both fronts. Steam can loosen congestion: sitting in a bathroom with the shower running for ten to fifteen minutes, a few times a day, works as a simple humidifier. And because appetite loss is often about smell rather than hunger, warming up canned food slightly, or offering something strongly scented like tuna or a favorite wet food, can be enough to get a congested cat eating again.</p>

<p>What doesn't help: cold medicine made for people. Don't give your cat anything from your own medicine cabinet. Many common human cold remedies, including some decongestants and pain relievers, are toxic to cats even in small doses. If a cat needs medication, it needs to be something a vet has specifically approved for cats. Lysine supplementation is a popular home remedy that gets recommended a lot online, but it's worth knowing that the evidence for it has become shakier over time. The current veterinary view, reflected in Cornell's own material, is that lysine's benefit for herpesvirus is unproven and possibly nonexistent, so don't rely on it as your main strategy even if a bottle of it is sitting on a pet store shelf labeled for this exact purpose.</p>

<h2>Why Some Cats Keep Getting This, Over and Over</h2>

<p>This is the part that surprises a lot of owners: herpesvirus infection in cats is often lifelong. VCA puts the number at around 80% of infected cats becoming chronic carriers after their first bout. The virus doesn't fully clear; it goes dormant in nerve tissue, the same general trick human herpesviruses use, and it can reactivate later, usually when the cat is stressed. Surgery, a new pet in the house, boarding, moving, or even a change in routine can be enough to trigger a flare-up months or years after the original infection.</p>

<p>Calicivirus behaves a little differently. Roughly half of infected cats become carriers, and for most of those, shedding fades out within a few months. A smaller share stay carriers for life, usually without showing symptoms themselves, but still capable of infecting other cats they come into contact with.</p>

<p>In practical terms, this means a cat who had a bad cold-like illness as a kitten may develop a crusty eye or a few days of sneezing years later, out of nowhere, especially during a stressful stretch, and it's a recurrence of the same original infection rather than a brand-new one. If you know your cat has had herpesvirus before, keeping stress down during big life changes, a move, a new pet, a boarding stay, isn't just comfort for its own sake. It's one of the more effective ways to reduce how often the virus comes back.</p>

<h2>The Bottom Line</h2>

<p>"Cat cold" is a convenient phrase, but it's describing a different illness from the one you get. Feline herpesvirus and feline calicivirus don't jump to humans, and human rhinoviruses don't jump to cats, so neither of you is giving the other one anything. What your cat has is feline upper respiratory infection: usually mild, usually self-limiting within a week or two with some basic supportive care, but worth a vet visit in kittens, older cats, cats with other health conditions, or any time breathing, eating, or an eye looks seriously affected. And if your cat is a carrier from a past infection, the sneezing fit that shows up during a stressful week isn't a mystery. It's the same old virus, waking back up.</p>

